ge of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }\\text{ }1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2}){e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}(x+y)^{2}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{u}^{2}{e}^{v}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{u}^{2}{e}^{v}|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{u}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{v}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{u}^{2}[{e}^{v}]_{0}^{2}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{u}^{2}\\cdot ({e}^{2}-1)\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{26}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{13({e}^{2}-1)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186481434 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1db0d9f5-1143-5b9b-b945-fa7c0b7aaf13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id 17592186480801} {:answer/id #uuid "5d785d71-3068-5db5-be3e-a22f94c4720c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id 17592186480912} {:answer/id #uuid "67c466f7-5e58-5990-9bfa-36a6b7e3de81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186480930} {:answer/id #uuid "bd2f30f4-4c96-59d7-80a8-398429f8a06c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id 17592186481101} {:answer/id #uuid "dc8d324b-b300-53c5-91d4-de7ddf595c6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186481157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67c466f7-5e58-5990-9bfa-36a6b7e3de81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186480930} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba1ace49-0ce8-5f3b-a500-296b7a164c42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186481306}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f47eca8f-5f35-574c-b95e-5dc9a27cb04d" :question/math-academy-id "q-160916" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y-x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le x+y\\le \\pi \\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0\\le y-x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le x+y\\le \\pi ,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le v\\le \\pi \\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ \\text{ }\\text{ }\\text{ }1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot [-\\operatorname{cos}⁡v]_{0}^{\\pi }\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot 2\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,du \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{sin}⁡u]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186495059 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76a2fca9-a004-56dc-bcb9-7a9d56bf96b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186494570} {:answer/id #uuid "807c89b0-1baf-5d8a-afd4-cecf678b8f6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡6" :db/id 17592186494600} {:answer/id #uuid "83e55904-4dad-5f50-9ed8-a699c5c350a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}" :db/id 17592186494608} {:answer/id #uuid "b4dca2c1-f3a5-573b-82d7-9841c885f465" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id 17592186494739} {:answer/id #uuid "ebf26341-3745-5f43-a14c-cda67d69e390" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id 17592186494880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83e55904-4dad-5f50-9ed8-a699c5c350a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}" :db/id 17592186494608} :answer-field/id #uuid "91b5b4ea-2fbf-58c6-b385-fcfece3b9311" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186494987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11be1c1c-7eab-52eb-aab8-c16c55806ef6" :question/math-academy-id "q-160917" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{y-2x}{y+2x}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y-2x\\le 2,\\,2\\le y+2x\\le 6\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0\\le y-2x\\le 2,\\,2\\le y+2x\\le 6,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-2x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=y+2x.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,2\\le v\\le 6\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ \\text{ }\\text{ }\\text{ }2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y-2x}{y+2x}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}|-\\frac{1}{4}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u[{\\int }_{2}^{6}\\frac{1}{v}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|v|]_{2}^{6}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{4}{[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186495100 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36ad6cac-d25a-50df-a71c-ff5530463782" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-4\\pi " :db/id 17592186494431} {:answer/id #uuid "377131c4-9276-5492-90f4-5fe9f9ea094b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2\\pi }" :db/id 17592186494433} {:answer/id #uuid "389c158d-eca4-5138-ade2-702a2174ac43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(2\\pi -1)" :db/id 17592186494436} {:answer/id #uuid "76f8296f-42f1-5d17-a5c6-7274b014898c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{12}{\\pi }" :db/id 17592186494571} {:answer/id #uuid "adfbcfe0-1ed2-552d-9eab-c92cbe93246a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6\\pi }" :db/id 17592186494723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76f8296f-42f1-5d17-a5c6-7274b014898c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{12}{\\pi }" :db/id 17592186494571} :answer-field/id #uuid "caaee621-928c-5865-8689-d0a675bcc7ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186495012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "75903bf3-d1b7-5813-a3cf-49663583fcbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-160915" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi xy)\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le xy\\le 4,\\,1\\le \\frac{y}{x}\\le 5\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le xy\\le 4,\\,1\\le \\frac{y}{x}\\le 5,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=xy\\,\\text{and}\\,v=\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,1\\le v\\le 5\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\text{ }\\text{ }\\text{ }y & x \\\\ -\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2y}{x})}=\\frac{1}{2v}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}{(\\frac{y}{x})}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u)|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u)|\\frac{1}{2v}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u)[{\\int }_{1}^{5}v\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u){[\\frac{1}{2}\\,{v}^{2}]}_{1}^{5}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u)\\cdot 12\\,du \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u)\\,du \\\\  & =6{[-\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi u)]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{\\pi }(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Cases When the Transformation Is Inferred From the Domain"}
